0. Env:
- JVM(vm that executes bytecode) < JRE(class lib for runtime env) < JDK(+dev tools)
- source code(.java) -> compile(javac compiler creates .class bytecode) -> run(starts jvm & converts to machine code & calls main)
- due to specific jvm -- its WORA
- maven vs gradle: simple, easy, slow, old, default vs faster, complex, hard, new

1. In one .java file:
- we can have only one public class 
- it should match the file name 
- and it cant be static
- static means belongs to class - shared by obj - so call without obj -- nested class, block, method and variable
- final means cant be reassigned -- variable, method, parameter, class

2. public static void main(string[] args):
- public: because jvm can call from outside 
- static: because jvm can call directly without obj creation
- void: doesn't return anything
- main: this is the starting point, can be overloaded
- string[] args: cli args

3. oops:
- interface: what it can do(unrelated class) - implement many
- abstract: what it is(close relation) - extend to one -- contract on subclasses
- ref type(Acc) and Obj type(SA) -- this pattern for generic type fn call

4. features:
- abs: fn exposed instead of same logic everywhere which can go wrong in change or order, so one place
- encap: method and variable in one class, access modifiers
- poly: dynamic(fn overriding - runtime) and static(fn overloading - compile time)
- inher: generic call using interface so accepted type 

5. solid:
- S: dont have many resp in same class, hard to test, change
- O: open for new, closed for existing
- L: subs with sibling class obj, shouldnt break the behavior
- I: no fat interface such that it makes class start throwing error
- D: DI is one pattern that satisfies DIP — you inject the abstraction from outside instead of creating it inside

6. spring:
- without spring: manual DI + in all .java file
- with spring(manual): wiring moved to single file + bean + context.getBean(Service.class)
- with spring(auto): component scan + component + but still context.getBean(Service.class)*
- with springboot: no config file, only components directly + no need of context.getBean(Service.class) + configure MVC

(not needed in case of web?)
- Why getBean()? Because your main() method is outside Spring's bean management. 
- You need a way to obtain the first Spring-managed object. Once you're inside Spring-managed objects, constructor injection handles the dependencies.
- so in case of non mvc, both may requires getBean and in case of mvc, none requires getBean 
- as this is dependant on startup, may require because other ways like event handler to bypass

7. structure:
Type                     | What triggers execution? | Typical entry point             | Needs web server?
-------------------------+--------------------------+---------------------------------+------------------
Standalone / CLI         | User runs program        | main()                          | No
Web MVC / REST API       | HTTP request             | @GetMapping, @PostMapping       | Yes
Scheduled application    | Time / cron              | @Scheduled                      | No
Message-driven worker    | Kafka / RabbitMQ message | @KafkaListener, @RabbitListener | No
Batch application        | Job execution            | Spring Batch Job                | No
Event-driven application | Application event        | @EventListener                  | No
WebSocket application    | WebSocket message        | @MessageMapping                 | Yes, usually
Library / SDK            | Another program calls it | Public methods                  | No